the number of children who have died  from strep a across the UK since  September has now risen to 19. our  science correspondent Thomas Moore has  the details  this is an update on the numbers from  the health Security Agency and they say  that there have been 16 in a deaths  under the age of 18 in England another  three uh in Wales and Northern Ireland  and they've also said that so far in  this season and the season starts in in  mid-september there have been 74 deaths  in England so they're not orphaned off  of adults who are dying from strep a too  and they've also given us some numbers  on the previous bad year for  streptococcus a there's bacteria that  really is moving around the country very  quickly back in  2017-18 which was the last bad year  there were 355 deaths across the whole  season and 27 of those were under the  age of 18. and and also they've been  encouraging people to to come forward if  people do have what they call strep  throat or scarlet fever  um they're making the point that these  are very easily treated with antibiotics  now just to remind you on the symptoms  of a strep throat that is a fever sore  throat swollen glands and a headache  scarlet fever generally a childhood  illness which has a characteristic  sandpapery rash and a swollen tongue  encouraging people to contact their GP  or nhs11 at an early stage so that those  children can be assessed there's also  been a change to the regulations over  prescriptions today something called a  serious shortage protocol that allows  pharmacists to prescribe an alternative  antibiotic if the antibiotic that's  written on the prescription by the GP  has run out and and that should make it  much easier for parents to to get a hold  of antibiotics really quickly
 

